---
title: Plugins
summary: The features beyond regions ship as their own packages. Install one, list its plugin once beside ogygia() in your Vite config, and the build wires its server and browser halves in. The package list, and the plugin API the first-party packages are built on.
related:
  - reference/api
  - start/install
  - reference/packaging
---

The core package, `ogygia`, is the region system: islands, lakes, holes, held regions, the client router and the server handle. Every other feature is its own package: content collections, flags, frozen pages, federation, the server router, the devtools dock and the profiler. You install only what you use. An app that never reads a flag carries none of the flag code.

## Turn a feature on

Install the package. Then list its plugin once in the `plugins` of your Vite config, beside `ogygia()`. Each plugin is a Vite plugin of its own, and `ogygia()` finds it there. Each factory comes from `<package>/vite`:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i @ogygia/flags @ogygia/freeze
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add @ogygia/flags @ogygia/freeze
```

:::

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { flags } from '@ogygia/flags/vite';
import { freeze } from '@ogygia/freeze/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    flags(),
    freeze({ ttl: 600 }),
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

`ogygia()` comes first, the feature plugins after it, and `sveltekit()` last. That is the only place a plugin is named. `ogygia.handle()` in `hooks.server.ts` already runs every listed plugin's server half, and the page runtime boots every browser half. Nothing else changes in your app.

A few rules hold for every plugin:

- **Call the factory.** `flags()`, not `flags`. The build says so if you forget.
- **Once each.** A plugin listed twice is a build error.
- **Only with `ogygia()`.** A plugin listed without `ogygia()` fails the build, and says so. The old `plugins` option of `ogygia()` is gone: passing it is a build error that names the new spelling.
- **Options are plain JSON.** They reach the plugin's server half at run time. Live objects (a store client, a vendor SDK) are set up in `hooks.server.ts`, through the package's own API.
- **List it only where you want it.** The list is code, so a plugin can depend on the mode: `...(mode !== 'production' ? [devtools()] : [])`.

## The packages

| Package              | Plugin                           | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `@ogygia/content`    | `content({ markdown, presets })` | [Content collections](/docs/content/collections), markdown (mdsvex + Shiki), the docs shell and search. Its svelte config helpers `preprocess()` / `extensions()` come from `@ogygia/content/vite` too. |
| `@ogygia/flags`      | `flags()`                        | [Flags & experiments](/docs/app/experiments): `flag()`, `decide()`. `flag()` decides natively without the plugin. A `decide({ source, exposure })` needs it.                                            |
| `@ogygia/freeze`     | `freeze({ ttl, default })`       | [Frozen pages](/docs/data-state/freeze): pages stored whole and served as bytes.                                                                                                                        |
| `@ogygia/federation` | `federation()`                   | [Fragment federation](/docs/app/federation): `federate()`, `mount()`.                                                                                                                                   |
| `@ogygia/router`     | `router()`                       | The [server router](/docs/app/server-router): routes as values. The plugin links the CSS of the pages it renders.                                                                                       |
| `@ogygia/devtools`   | `devtools({ dock, profiler })`   | The [devtools dock](/docs/devtools/overview) and the [SSR profiler](/docs/profiler/overview).                                                                                                           |
| `@ogygia/testing`    | none                             | [Test helpers](/docs/devtools/testing) for Playwright. Import it in tests.                                                                                                                              |
| `@ogygia/cli`        | none                             | The command: `npx ogygia init`, `ogygia mcp`, the AI skill. `npx ogygia …` runs it for you.                                                                                                             |

Every package takes `ogygia` as a peer dependency, and they release together, at one version. Keep them on the same version as `ogygia`.

## The plugin API

::: warning First-party, not stable
The API below is what the packages above are built on. It is public so you can read how they work, but it is **first-party only for now**. Its shape can change in any release, with no notice. Do not build a published plugin on it yet.
:::

A plugin is three pieces, each in its own file, so server code never reaches a browser bundle:

| Piece                | Where                                                                  | Made with                                                      |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| The **plugin**       | the package's `./vite` export, listed beside `ogygia()` in vite.config | `definePlugin` from `ogygia/vite`, which returns a Vite plugin |
| The **server half**  | a module the plugin names in `server`                                  | `defineServer` from `ogygia/plugin`                            |
| The **browser half** | a module the plugin names in `client`                                  | `defineClient` from `ogygia/plugin/client`                     |

The plugin **names its halves**: `server` and `client` hold each module's location, resolved next to the plugin's own file with `import.meta.resolve`. Either one may be missing. They are files of the package itself, so the plugin's modules exist once, and they need no entry in the package's `exports`: those stay yours to name. The build imports them statically: nothing is looked up at run time, and a feature with no plugin listed costs zero bytes.

### The plugin: `definePlugin`

```ts title=src/vite.ts
import { definePlugin, type OgygiaPlugin } from 'ogygia/vite';

export function freeze(options: { ttl?: number } = {}): OgygiaPlugin<{ ttl: number }> {
  return definePlugin({
    name: '@ogygia/freeze',            // the package name: its identity
    server: import.meta.resolve('./server.ts'),
    options: { ttl: options.ttl ?? 86400 },
    build(b) {
      b.routeOption('freeze');         // pages may say `export const freeze = false`
    }
  });
}
```

| Field      | What it is                                                                                                                                                            |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `name`     | The package name. It must resolve from the app.                                                                                                                       |
| `server`   | The server half's location: `import.meta.resolve('./server.ts')`. A built package ships `./server.js` beside the plugin's file, and its build rewrites the extension. |
| `client`   | The browser half's location, the same way.                                                                                                                            |
| `options`  | Plain JSON. The server half reads it as `app.options` and `ctx.options`.                                                                                              |
| `enforce`  | `'pre'` or `'post'`: run before or after the other plugins, in every realm. Otherwise, list order.                                                                    |
| `build(b)` | Build-time contributions, with the build context `b`.                                                                                                                 |

`definePlugin` returns a Vite plugin. It carries the definition under a symbol of its own (`Symbol.for('ogygia.plugin')`). That is how `ogygia()` finds it in the `plugins` list, so a plugin's name needs no convention: its package name is enough.

#### Vite hooks of its own

A plugin that needs Vite hooks of its own returns an array: its `definePlugin(...)` and a plain Vite plugin beside it. Vite flattens the array, so the app still lists one `yaml()`. `content()` and `devtools()` work this way.

```ts title=src/vite.ts
import { definePlugin } from 'ogygia/vite';

export function yaml() {
  return [
    definePlugin({ name: 'ogygia-yaml', server: import.meta.resolve('./server.ts') }),
    { name: 'ogygia-yaml:transform', transform(code, id) { /* a plain Vite plugin */ } }
  ];
}
```

### The build context: `b`

`build(b)` runs once, when Vite loads its config. `b.root` is the app's root and `b.dev` is `true` under `vite dev`.

| Member                              | What it asks the build for                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `routeOption(name)`                 | A per-route boolean, `export const <name> = true` in a page or layout file. It cascades like `csr`, is stripped before Kit sees the file, and is read back on the server with `ctx.routeOption(name)`. Kit's own names are refused. |
| `foreignHydrate()`                  | Keep the exports that let another app's page wake this app's islands (fragment federation). Off by default: it costs every island a little.                                                                                         |
| `macro(name, handler)`              | An `import.meta.og.<name>(…)` construct this plugin compiles. The handler gets the module's code and returns the new code. Core's own macros (`wire`, `$`, `source`, `store`, `bake`) cannot be claimed.                            |
| `markup(source)`                    | A file format that becomes Svelte through a preprocessor (markdown). It hands back the region transform to call on the preprocessed code, so the format's islands are found.                                                        |
| `events({ lazy })`                  | Compile the framework's events in, browser and server (`ogygia/events`). With no plugin asking, every emit folds away. `lazy`: measure only once someone opens a tool.                                                              |
| `measure()`                         | Record the build's measurement data (region detail, chunk contents, source maps, the module map): what a profiler reads.                                                                                                            |
| `files({ root, dirs, files })`      | Compile a package's own files as app source: the regions of a UI the plugin serves.                                                                                                                                                 |
| `pageModules({ importers, files })` | Modules that render pages outside Kit's routes. Every component they import gets its CSS linked when it renders.                                                                                                                    |

### The server half: `defineServer`

```ts title=src/server.ts
import { defineServer } from 'ogygia/plugin';

export default defineServer<{ ttl: number }, { hit: boolean }>({
  setup(app) { /* once: app.options, app.notify, app.ask */ },
  state: () => ({ hit: false }),                  // fresh per request: ctx.state
  async request(ctx) { /* every request */ },
  async page(ctx) { /* a page request: return a Response to answer it */ },
  async response(ctx, res) { /* return a Response to replace the page's */ },
  done(ctx) { /* the request ended */ },
  async invalidate(tags) { /* a shared hook */ }
});
```

Each hook has one kind. The kind says what its return value does.

| Hook                 | Kind        | When it runs                                                                                                                                                                      |
| -------------------- | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `setup(app)`         | once        | When the server starts.                                                                                                                                                           |
| `state()`            | per request | Makes this plugin's `ctx.state` for one request.                                                                                                                                  |
| `handle(ctx, next)`  | around      | Wraps the whole request, like Kit's `handle`. `next()` runs the rest and returns its response. `next(options)` passes Kit `resolve()` options. The first plugin is the outermost. |
| `request(ctx)`       | first       | Every request, before the split into pages and region requests. The first Response answers it.                                                                                    |
| `page(ctx)`          | first       | A page request, before Kit renders. The first Response answers it (a stored copy).                                                                                                |
| `response(ctx, res)` | sequential  | The page's response, before it streams out. A Response replaces it.                                                                                                               |
| `done(ctx)`          | parallel    | The request's end, always, even when it failed or was answered early. It must not throw and must not wait on anything slow.                                                       |

**Shared hooks** are how plugins talk to each other. A plugin implements one as a method of its server half. Anyone calls it through `app`:

- `app.notify.<hook>(…)`: every plugin that has it runs. It resolves when all are done.
- `app.ask.<hook>(…)`: the first answer that is not `undefined`, in plugin order.

Core owns the common names: `invalidate(tags)` (drop stored pages with these tags), `invalidated(tags)` (stored pages were dropped) and `lastBuild(version)`. Nobody implementing a hook is normal: `notify` does nothing and `ask` answers `undefined`. A plugin's own hooks carry its name, and are typed by declaration merging:

```ts
declare module 'ogygia/plugin' {
  interface ServerHooks { myPluginReset(path: string): void }
}
```

### The contexts: `app` and `ctx`

`app` is the server process, as one plugin sees it. `setup` gets it, and every `ctx.app` is it.

| Member                                         | What it is                                                                                                  |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `name` · `options`                             | The plugin's name and its options from vite.config.                                                         |
| `notify` · `ask`                               | Call the shared hooks (above).                                                                              |
| `facts(request)` · `current()`                 | A request's facts. `current()` finds the request the running code belongs to (a load, a render), or `null`. |
| `sign(message)` · `verify(message, signature)` | The app's region key, as HMAC. The key itself is never handed out.                                          |
| `devtools(event)`                              | Record an event into the current request's devtools stream. A no-op unless devtools is on for that browser. |
| `warn(message)`                                | Warn once per message, prefixed with the plugin's name.                                                     |

`ctx` is one request, as one plugin sees it. `handle` gets a smaller one: `event`, `options` and `app`.

| Member                      | What it is                                                                         |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `event`                     | Kit's own `RequestEvent`, untouched.                                               |
| `path`                      | The decoded pathname.                                                              |
| `state` · `options` · `app` | This plugin's per-request state, its options, its app.                             |
| `facts`                     | The request's shared facts (below).                                                |
| `routeOption(name)`         | This request's value for a route option, or `undefined` when nothing declares it.  |
| `renderHole(endpoint)`      | Render one signed hole in place, with this request's cookies. Its HTML, or `null`. |
| `perVisitor(reason)`        | Say this render belongs to one visitor. The first reason is kept.                  |

### Request facts

Facts are what one request has learned, shared by every plugin and by core. Whoever learns a fact writes it. Whoever needs it reads it. Neither imports the other.

| Fact          | Meaning                                                                                                      |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `storable`    | This render may be stored and served to others later. Its holes get long-lived URLs.                         |
| `perVisitor`  | The first reason this render is per-visitor (a flag read, a streamed promise, a personal cookie), or `null`. |
| `streamed`    | The page streams load promises after the document.                                                           |
| `private`     | The response belongs to one browser (devtools is on for it). Never store or share it.                        |
| `answerForm`  | An app transform renders this router-fetched page in a form a store keyed by path does not hold.             |
| `sources`     | Data-source receipts the render filed, collected only once a plugin sets a list here.                        |
| `assignments` | Every flag decided for this request, name to variant.                                                        |

A plugin adds its own facts the same way it adds hooks, with `declare module 'ogygia/plugin' { interface RequestFacts { … } }`.

### The browser half: `defineClient`

```ts title=src/client.ts
import { defineClient } from 'ogygia/plugin/client';

export default defineClient({
  boot({ kitPage }) { /* once, before the first region wakes */ },
  regionMount(el, { entry, urls }) { /* a region's component started */ },
  regionUnmount(el, { entry }) { /* …and left the page */ }
});
```

| Hook                               | When it runs                                                                                                                                          |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `boot({ kitPage })`                | Once, as the runtime boots, before any region wakes. `kitPage` is `true` on a `csr = true` page, where Kit hydrates and the region hooks do not fire. |
| `regionMount(el, { entry, urls })` | A region's component started in the browser. `entry` is its component's stable module URL, `urls` the files it runs from.                             |
| `regionUnmount(el, { entry })`     | A region's component left the page.                                                                                                                   |

The browser half is in the boot's static graph, so it ships to every page. Keep it small, and load anything heavy with a dynamic import of its own, on demand. With no browser half in the build, core's calls into these hooks fold away.

### Helpers

| Import                  | What it holds                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `ogygia/plugin`         | `defineServer`, the types (`ServerApp`, `RequestContext`, `RequestFacts`, `ServerHooks`), region values (`region`, `htmlRegion`, `deferredRegion`), route options for a programmatic router (`registerRouteOptions`), late regions (`openLateScope`, `takeLateRegions`, `lateSlot`, `lateChunk`). |
| `ogygia/plugin/client`  | `defineClient` and its types.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| `ogygia/plugin/utils`   | Pure helpers with no context, safe in any bundle: `fnv1a32`, `escapeAttr`, `escapeText`, `SizedLru`, `sourceTag`, the Standard Schema types.                                                                                                                                                      |
| `ogygia/plugin/css`     | `componentCssHead(components)`: the CSS head tags for components a programmatic router rendered.                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| `ogygia/plugin/testing` | `startPlugin(server, { name, options })`: run a server half in a unit test, with no server.                                                                                                                                                                                                       |

```ts title=test/server.test.ts
import { startPlugin } from 'ogygia/plugin/testing';
import server from '../src/server.ts';

const { ctx } = startPlugin(server, { name: '@ogygia/flags' });
await server.request?.(ctx(new Request('http://localhost/')));
```
